  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nNear-record to record high temperatures are occurring across \nthe region this afternoon. As of 2 PM, the temperature at O'Hare\n(ORD) reached 68 degrees, tying the daily record. Meanwhile, \nRockford (RFD) reached 67 degrees, just short of its 69-degree \nrecord. Temperatures could rise another degree or two before 4 \nPM, potentially breaking both records. \n\nTemperatures will drop into the 50s this evening, with \novernight lows holding in the mid-to-upper 40s to set up an \nunseasonably warm start to Saturday. Mostly sunny conditions \nwill bring another mild day on Saturday. Record highs for \nSaturday are slightly higher (72 at ORD and 73 at RFD) and may \nremain just out of reach. Nonetheless, temperatures could \napproach 70 degrees, a threshold reached only a few times this \nlate in the year. \n\nA cold front will cross the area on Sunday, ending the period \nof near-record warmth for the foreseeable future. Much cooler \nair will settle in behind the front for next week, accompanied \nby a band of rain along the boundary on Sunday. The highest rain\nchances are expected during the morning into early afternoon \nacross northern Illinois, and from late morning through mid-\nafternoon across northwestern Indiana. Conditions will clear \nfrom northwest to southeast by Sunday evening. Breezy \nnortheasterly winds behind the front will push temperatures down\ninto the 40s by early Sunday evening, with overnight lows \nfalling into the 20s and lower 30s. \n\nMuch cooler conditions will be the main weather story through \nthe holiday week. High temperatures will top out mostly in the \n40s, with overnight lows dropping into the 20s. Generally dry \nweather is expected early next week. Forecast trends will need \nto be monitored for the next upper-level trough/low expected to \ntrack into the lower Great Lakes and Ohio Valley mid to late \nweek. Some ensemble solutions suggest precipitation (primarily \nsnow) could clip the area on Wednesday, though any activity will\nface a very dry antecedent air mass.\n\nKJB",
